A||N||9||Voytek||Timing Cover Gasket - has anyone done this? How?||||1||
Z||000000||Voytek||11-07-2001||10:29 AM||||Ok.  Here's the deal.  My '85 SE (2.8) is leaking some oil and antifreeze from the timing cover.  I have done the following so far:<BR>- removed the oil pan (changing this gasket too)<BR>- removed the water pump<BR>- removed a few odds and ends on that side of the engine<P>I have looked in the Haynes manual but the automotive lingo they use is slightly beyond me.  They talk about removing the harmonic balancer and something about not screwing up the engine balancing.<P>Does anyone know the procedure in simpler terms?  Is it easy?<P>One other question.  Do I use some kind of RTV sealant on top of the gaskets?  The oil pan gasket is cork - do I need to use a sealant with it too?<P>Thanks for your help.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Voytek (edited 11-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-07-2001||12:46 PM||||The harmonic balancer is the rotating thing with the (ignition) timing mark on it.  To remove it, take off the crank pulley(s), and take out the bolt+washer in the center of the crank pulley.  To do this, you'll probably have to immobilize the crank to keep it from turning with the bolt.<P>Pull the balancer off the end of the crank using a steering wheel puller.  The harmonic balancer is keyed to the crank, so you pretty much can't screw up the indexing to the crank (i.e.- you won't mess up the balance).  Just don't lose the key, if it falls out.<P>To reinstall the harmonic balancer, I pulled it on using the crank center bolt+washer (the 1st two items mentioned above). <P>I seem to remember that you <B>have</B> to use RTV on the pan/timing cover interface.  I don't know anything about the pan gasket itself - I've never had to mess with mine.<P>Hope this helps, and good luck to you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">            ||||reg||1
Z||000002||SKIDMARK||11-07-2001||01:49 PM||||Just an addition to the above instructions. When you put the harmonic balancer back on, put a little RTV in the keyway on the balancer. This point was the sorce of a frustrating oil leak I had on a 3.1. It flung oil all over the engine compartment. <P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||11-07-2001||02:02 PM||||Thanks guys.  Those are some pretty straight forward instructions.  I'm not sure what you mean by 'keyed' to the crank but I'm sure I'll find out once I take this thing off.<P>One more question.  What if the key falls out?  Is it possible to screw up the balancer's position then?  Should the position be marked?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||11-07-2001||04:27 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B><P>One more question.  What if the key falls out?  Is it possible to screw up the balancer's position then?  Should the position be marked?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Not to worry, just pick it up and put it back. You'll see once you take it apart.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 11-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Voytek||11-07-2001||04:40 PM||||Awesome.  Thanks guys.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||avengador1||11-07-2001||07:56 PM||||I don't know if this helps but you can just about see where the key goes on the bottom of the crank near the oil pan. I used Indian head gasket sealer on both sides of the gaskets to assemble them and have no leaks. Hope this helps. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero13.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||avengador1||11-07-2001||07:59 PM||||Actually the keyway slot is on the top side of the crank shaft(I looked at the picture again). I used the gasket sealer on both sides of the cork gaskets. It helps to keep them in place during assembly.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Back On Holiday||11-07-2001||09:59 PM||||I personally DO NOT use Cork Gaskets, they LEAK.  I use the orange hi temp silicone sealant to seal the oilpan(among other things).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Voytek||11-08-2001||10:13 AM||||Well, the original gasket on the pan was cork.  It did well for almost 16 years so it may not be that bad.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks for all the help.  I will use sealer on both sides of gaskets.  The job doesn't seem so bad now that I have a description in English (as compared to Haynes).||||reg||1
